Assistant: It seems the imported component is an async component (likely a Next.js page component). Let's modify the code to properly handle the async nature of the component using React.lazy and async component handling:

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/SamlIdpPage-5a6274b8.tsx">
  <change>
    <description>Handle async page component properly with useEffect and state</description>
    <content>
import React, { Suspense, useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import ImportedComponent from '../../app/(use-page-wrapper)/auth/saml-idp/page';

function SamlIdpPageWrapper({ params, searchParams }) {
  const [PageComponent, setPageComponent] = useState(null);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const loadComponent = async () => {
      try {
        // Handle both cases where ImportedComponent might be a promise or a component
        const Component = await Promise.resolve(ImportedComponent);
        const ResolvedComponent = Component.default || Component;
        setPageComponent(() => ResolvedComponent);
      } catch (err) {
        setError(err);
        console.error("Error loading SamlIdpPage:", err);
      }
    };

    loadComponent();
  }, []);

  if (error) {
    return <div>Error loading component</div>;
  }

  if (!PageComponent) {
    return <div>Loading...</div>;
  }

  return <PageComponent params={params} searchParams={searchParams} />;
}

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    params: {
      type: "string",
      value: "{}",
      label: "Params",
    },
    searchParams: {
      type: "string",
      value: '{"code": "example_code"}',
      label: "Search Params",
    },
  });

  const params = JSON.parse(state.params.value);
  const searchParams = JSON.parse(state.searchParams.value);

  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
      <SamlIdpPageWrapper params={params} searchParams={searchParams} />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
    </content>
  </change>
</file>

I've made several important changes to handle the async component properly:

1. Added useState and useEffect hooks to manage the async component loading
2. Created a proper loading mechanism that waits for the component to be resolved
3. Added error handling for the component loading process
4. Properly handle both cases where ImportedComponent might be a promise or a regular component
5. Added proper state management for the loaded component
6. Maintained the Suspense boundary for any other async operations

The changes address the "[object Promise]" error by:
- Properly resolving the Promise before rendering
- Using state management to render the component only after it's fully loaded
- Handling both default and named exports
- Providing appropriate loading and error states

This solution ensures that we're not trying to render a Promise directly in the React tree, which was causing the original error.

